================================================================================
OBSERVATIONS (neither is a functional failure; both are TFS-wording divergences)
================================================================================
O-1  S1566.1.04 says a server that finds an address in use "will repeat this whole process for
     the next available address". Measured: the DUT abandons the address, records it in
     `show ip dhcp binding` with an empty ClientId, and sends NOTHING further for that
     DISCOVER — the client gets no OFFER at all and must re-send DISCOVER, which then succeeds
     on the next address (leg 3a vs 3b). This is stock ISC dhcpd behaviour and harmless with a
     real RFC 2131 client, which always retries. The spec's wording implies a roll-on inside
     the same transaction, which does not happen. Evidence: work/leg3.json, work/leg3b.json,
     work/leg3-dut.log.

O-2  S1566.1.01 step 2 says the server waits T ms "for a response to the ARP Request or ICMP
     Echo Request", and S1566.1.04 says probing exits and the address is not offered if a
     response is received. Measured under probe type PING: an ARP response alone did NOT stop
     the offer — the DUT escalated to ICMP as specified, got no echo reply, and offered the
     address (leg 5). Only the ICMP echo reply withheld it (leg 6). The behaviour is coherent
     and arguably the right design (under ping probing the ping is the authority), but it does
     not match the literal "or" in S1566.1.01. Evidence: work/leg5.pcap, work/leg6.pcap.

Neither observation let the server hand out an address whose authoritative probe was answered.
Both are offered to the Test Engineer as candidate CR/spec-clarification material; grading them
is not the tester's call.

